{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794184441,"nonce":"nxAH3Lr275Ef5b3XaXTU4sRolm0=","message":"Owner direction, 2026-11-09: borrow HXSL's export, import and reuse of shader code (hlwgpu, haxe/hlwgpu/hxsl). What it does and how each part maps to russl:\n\n1. Modules are shaders without entry points. In HXSL a shader block with no vertex, fragment or compute function compiles to nothing; it exists to be imported. In russl a module holds pub fns, structs, consts and bindings, emits no artifact, and is reused by path.\n\n2. Import by path, transitively. HXSL's `import(Path)` and `extends(Path)` (Checker.hx) load the target's stored source and check it into the current shader, recursing into its own imports. The Haxe macro finds the source with Context.getType, reading metadata the target's build macro left on its class. A Rust proc macro cannot read another item. The standard way around that is the macro callback:\n   - Every russl module also expands to a hidden macro_rules (`__russl_module_\u003cname\u003e!`, exported with `pub use` so a path reaches it across crates) that hands its own token source to a callback.\n   - `russl!` meets `use crate::sdf::common::*;`, expands to a call of that module's macro with itself as the callback and the rest of its input carried along, and resumes once the tokens arrive. Each import is one round trip; nested imports recurse the same way.\n   - russl_build (the build.rs driver) reads files directly and needs none of this; the callback is what makes inline `russl!` modules importable from other crates.\n   `use` replaces `import`, with Rust's rules for paths and visibility; only `pub` items are importable. `extends` (inherit a shader's declarations and replace some of its functions) waits until a case needs it.\n\n3. One pipeline, reachable code only. HXSL checks, inlines helper calls, links, splits stages, removes dead code and prints (Compiler.hx). russl does the same over its IR before lowering to naga, so an import costs nothing it does not reach. Today's build.rs prelude subsetting in blinc_gpu does a text version of this; russl replaces it.\n\n4. Extensions keep framework pieces out of the core. HXSL's Extension (Extension.hx) adds extern functions, a prelude every shader of a family starts with, a pass over each checked shader, WGSL for extra builtins, and rules for uniform blocks and bind groups. russl gets a Rust trait with the same hooks, registered by the consuming crate. Blinc's Primitive ABI, the aux_data accessors and the tier variants (storage, data texture, vertex buffer) become a Blinc extension rather than russl features.\n\n5. Layout constants. HXSL adds WGSL plus SIZE, GROUP, BINDING, per-member offsets, vertex input locations, target locations and pipeline-constant keys to the shader class (Build.hx). russl emits the same as associated consts, alongside the generated Rust twins of the shader structs.\n\nEffect on the milestones: M1 gains modules and `use` within one russl invocation (no callback yet); M4 becomes the cross-module and cross-crate step (callback macro and russl_build) together with the extension trait, which the SDF tier variants need.","files":null}]}